.\" fast-highlight(5): configuration, theme, and command spec files.
.TH FAST-HIGHLIGHT 5 2026-10-04 "fast-highlight 0.1.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
fast-highlight \- configuration, theme, and command spec files for fast-highlight
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB config /config.toml
.br
.IB config /theme.toml
.br
.IB config /themes/ name .toml
.br
.IB config /specs/*.toml
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR fast-highlight (1)
reads three kinds of TOML file from its config directory, written
.I config
on this page:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR config.toml ,
the size limits, log settings, and theme name;
.IP \(bu 2
a theme, which maps token types to zsh highlight styles;
.IP \(bu 2
command specs, which declare the subcommands and options of individual commands.
.PP
The config directory is
.B $FAST_HIGHLIGHT_CONFIG_DIR
when that variable is set and not empty, else
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fast-highlight
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set to an absolute path, else
.BR $HOME/.config/fast-highlight .
.B fast-highlight check-config
prints the directory and validates every file in it.
.PP
Every file is optional.
A missing
.B config.toml
yields the defaults, a missing theme yields the built-in
.B truecolor
theme, and with no spec files
only the built-in specs apply.
.PP
The daemon reads
.B config.toml
and the spec files when it starts.
The plugin reads the theme, through
.BR "fast-highlight styles" ,
when it loads.
The zsh function
.B fasthl-reload
does both again, so it applies a change to any of these files to the current shell.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
.B config.toml
has one top-level key and two tables.
Every key is optional, and an unknown key is an error.
When the file has an error, the daemon uses the default configuration for every key and writes
the error to its log.
.TP
.BR theme " (string)"
The name of the theme to use, as described under
.BR "THEME SELECTION" .
A name is not empty, does not start with
.BR . ,
and does not contain
.BR / .
.SS The [limits] table
Sizes are measured in bytes of the text being highlighted, which is
.B PREBUFFER
followed by
.BR BUFFER .
.TP
.BR lex-only-bytes " (integer, default 10240)"
Above this size, the daemon skips command and filesystem lookups and reports only the syntax
of the text.
.TP
.BR hard-cap-bytes " (integer, default 65536)"
Above this size, the daemon returns no highlighting.
The value is greater than 0 and at least
.BR lex-only-bytes .
The plugin does not send a command line longer than
.B FASTHL_MAX_LENGTH
bytes, which defaults to the same 65536, so raising this limit has an effect only together with
that variable.
.TP
.BR max-spans " (integer, default 500)"
The most spans the daemon returns for one request.
A longer list is cut to its first
.B max-spans
spans.
The value is greater than 0.
.TP
.BR max-path-checks " (integer, default 64)"
The maximum number of filesystem checks per highlight request.
A cached result does not count against it.
Words beyond the limit are not checked.
.TP
.BR path-cache-ttl-ms " (integer, default 1000)"
How long a cached filesystem check result stays valid, in milliseconds.
.SS The [log] table
.TP
.BR timing " (boolean, default false)"
Writes one timing line per request to the daemon's log, as
.B serve \-\-timing
does.
.TP
.BR file " (string)"
The daemon's log file.
The value is an absolute path, or starts with
.B \(ti/
or is
.BR \(ti ,
which expand to the home directory.
.BI \(ti user
is not expanded and is an error.
A
.B \-\-log
option given to
.B fast-highlight serve
takes precedence.
When the key is absent, the daemon uses the default location given under
.B LOGGING
in
.BR fast-highlight (1).
.SH THEME SELECTION
When
.B config.toml
has no
.B theme
key, the theme is
.IB config /theme.toml
if that file exists, and the built-in
.B truecolor
theme otherwise.
.PP
When
.B theme
is set to
.IR name ,
the theme is
.IB config /themes/ name .toml
if that file exists, and the built-in theme called
.I name
otherwise.
It is an error when neither exists.
.PP
The built-in themes are:
.TP
.B default
Basic colours only, for dark and light terminals and for terminals without truecolor support.
.TP
.B truecolor
The default theme.
24-bit colours for dark terminals, inheriting from
.BR default .
It needs a terminal with truecolor support, or the
.B zsh/nearcolor
module on 88- and 256-colour terminals.
.PP
.B fast-highlight styles
prints the resolved styles of the active theme.
.SH THEME FILES
A theme file has these top-level keys, all optional.
An unknown key is an error.
.TP
.BR inherits " (string, default \(dqtruecolor\(dq)"
The theme this one is layered over: the name of a built-in theme, or
.B none
for an empty base.
A theme file cannot inherit from another theme file.
.TP
.B [meta]
Informational keys
.B name
and
.BR description ,
both strings.
They are checked for typos and otherwise ignored.
.TP
.B [styles]
One entry per token type, keyed by the token type names listed under
.BR "TOKEN TYPES" .
An entry replaces the inherited style of that token type.
An unknown token type is an error.
.PP
A style is either a string or a table.
.SS Style strings
A style string is a comma-separated list of items, in the syntax zsh 5.9 accepts in
.BR region_highlight .
Whitespace around each item is removed.
.TP
.BI fg= colour
The foreground colour.
.TP
.BI bg= colour
The background colour.
.TP
.BR bold ", " underline ", " standout
Attributes.
zsh 5.9 does not support italic or faint, and neither is accepted.
.TP
.B none
No styling.
It is valid only on its own.
.PP
A
.I colour
is one of
.BR black ,
.BR red ,
.BR green ,
.BR yellow ,
.BR blue ,
.BR magenta ,
.BR cyan ,
.BR white ,
and
.BR default ;
a number from 0 to 255; or
.BI # rgb
or
.BI # rrggbb
in hexadecimal.
Each item appears at most once in a style.
.PP
The empty string leaves the token type unstyled, removing any style it would inherit.
.SS Style tables
A style table has the keys
.B fg
and
.BR bg ,
each a colour string or an integer from 0 to 255, and
.BR bold ,
.BR underline ,
and
.BR standout ,
each a boolean.
An empty table leaves the token type unstyled.
The table
.B {\ fg\ =\ \(dqred\(dq,\ bold\ =\ true\ }
is the same style as the string
.BR \(dqfg=red,bold\(dq .
.SH TOKEN TYPES
These are the token types the daemon reports and that themes and the plugin's
.B FASTHL_STYLES
table style.
Spans nest: a string can contain a parameter, and the nested span is applied after the span that
contains it.
.TP
.B default
Plain text with no particular meaning.
It is rarely reported, since unstyled text needs no span.
.TP
.B error
A syntax error, an unknown command, an unknown subcommand, or an invalid option.
.TP
.B reserved-word
A reserved word in command position:
.BR if ,
.BR then ,
.BR for ,
.BR { ,
.BR [[ ,
.BR ! ,
and so on.
.TP
.B alias
A command word that names a regular alias.
.TP
.B suffix-alias
A command word that is resolved through a suffix alias
.RB ( "alias \-s" ).
.TP
.B global-alias
A word anywhere on the line that names a global alias
.RB ( "alias \-g" ).
.TP
.B function
A command word that names a shell function, or the name in a function definition.
.TP
.B builtin
A command word that names a shell builtin.
.TP
.B command
A command word that names an external command found in
.B PATH
or by an explicit path.
.TP
.B precommand
A precommand modifier or wrapper:
.BR sudo ,
.BR noglob ,
.BR nocorrect ,
.BR exec ,
.BR command ,
.BR builtin ,
.BR env ,
.BR time ,
and similar commands.
.TP
.B separator
A command separator or pipeline operator:
.BR ; ,
.BR & ,
.BR && ,
.BR || ,
.BR | ,
.BR |& ,
.BR &! ,
and
.BR &| .
.TP
.B redirection
A redirection operator with any file descriptor prefix:
.BR > ,
.BR 2> ,
.BR >&2 ,
.BR << ,
.BR <<< ,
.BR &> ,
and so on.
.TP
.B heredoc
A here-document delimiter word and the here-document body.
.TP
.B single-quoted
A single-quoted string,
.BR \(aq...\(aq .
.TP
.B double-quoted
A double-quoted string,
.BR \(dq...\(dq .
.TP
.B dollar-quoted
A dollar-quoted string,
.BR $\(aq...\(aq .
.TP
.B backquoted
A backquoted command substitution,
.BR \(ga...\(ga ,
delimiters included.
.TP
.B escape
A backslash escape, inside or outside quotes.
.TP
.B parameter
A parameter expansion:
.BR $name ,
.BR ${...} ,
.BR $1 ,
.BR $? ,
and so on.
.TP
.B substitution
The delimiters of a command substitution,
.B $(
and
.BR ) .
.TP
.B process-substitution
The delimiters of a process substitution,
.BR <( ,
.BR >( ,
or
.BR =( ,
and
.BR ) .
.TP
.B arithmetic
An arithmetic expansion or command,
.B $((...))\&
or
.BR ((...)) .
.TP
.B glob
Glob characters and extended glob operators:
.BR * ,
.BR ? ,
.BR [...] ,
.BR ** ,
.BR # ,
.BR ^ ,
and
.BR \(ti .
.TP
.B glob-qualifier
A glob qualifier list at the end of a word, such as
.B (.)\&
or
.BR (om[1,3]) .
.TP
.B brace-expansion
A brace expansion,
.B {a,b}
or
.BR {1..10} .
.TP
.B history-expansion
A history expansion:
.BR !! ,
.BR !$ ,
.BR !\-2 ,
.BR ^old^new ,
and so on.
.TP
.B comment
A comment.
Comments are recognised only when
.B INTERACTIVE_COMMENTS
is set.
.TP
.B assignment
The
.B name=
or
.B name+=
part of an assignment, and the parentheses of an array value.
.TP
.B grouping
Subshell and group delimiters
.B (
and
.BR ) ,
and the parentheses of a function definition.
.TP
.B operator
An operator inside
.BR "[[ ... ]]" :
.BR \-f ,
.BR == ,
.BR =\(ti ,
.BR && ,
.BR ! ,
.BR < ,
and so on.
.TP
.B path
An argument that names an existing file.
.TP
.B path-directory
An argument that names an existing directory.
.TP
.B path-prefix
An argument that is a prefix of an existing path, while it is still being typed.
.TP
.B subcommand
A subcommand known to the command's spec, such as
.B commit
in
.BR "git commit" .
.TP
.B option
An option known to the command's spec, such as
.B \-\-verbose
or
.BR \-C .
.SH COMMAND SPEC FILES
A command spec declares the subcommands and options of one command, so that the daemon reports
known ones as
.B subcommand
and
.B option
and, where the spec says its lists are complete, unknown ones as
.BR error .
Each spec is one TOML file.
The file name is irrelevant; the
.B name
key identifies the command.
.PP
The built-in specs are compiled into the binary.
They cover
.BR git ,
.BR cargo ,
.BR docker ,
.BR kubectl ,
.BR systemctl ,
and
.BR npm ,
and the precommands
.BR sudo ,
.BR doas ,
.BR env ,
.BR nice ,
.BR nohup ,
.BR time ,
.BR timeout ,
.BR stdbuf ,
.BR ionice ,
.BR chrt ,
.BR taskset ,
.BR noglob ,
.BR nocorrect ,
.BR exec ,
.BR command ,
.BR builtin ,
and
.BR \- .
The regular files whose names end in
.B .toml
in
.IB config /specs
are read after the built-ins, in file-name order.
.SS Top-level keys
.TP
.BR name " (string, required)"
The command name.
.TP
.BR aliases " (array of strings, default [])"
Other command names that share this spec.
.TP
.BR merge " (boolean, default false)"
In a user file: merge into the existing spec of the same name instead of replacing it.
.TP
.BR common-options " (array of strings, default [])"
Options valid at the top level and at every subcommand level, at any depth.
.TP
.BR precommand " (boolean, default false)"
The command runs another command, as
.BR sudo ,
.BR env ,
and
.B nice
do.
.TP
.BR skip-assignments " (boolean, default false)"
Precommands only:
.IB NAME = value
words before the wrapped command are skipped, as for
.BR env .
.TP
.BR positional " (integer, default 0)"
Precommands only: the number of plain arguments between the options and the wrapped command, as
in
.BI timeout " duration command" .
.PP
The top level also accepts every level key.
.SS Level keys
These keys are valid at the top level and in every
.BI [subcommands. name ]
table.
Subcommand tables nest to any depth, as in
.BR [subcommands.remote.subcommands.add] .
.TP
.BR options " (array of strings, default [])"
Options valid at this level only.
.TP
.BR subcommands " (table, default {})"
The subcommands of this level, keyed by name.
.TP
.BR aliases " (array of strings, default [])"
Subcommand tables only: other names for this subcommand, as
.B ps
is for
.BR "docker container ls" .
.TP
.BR complete " (boolean, default false)"
The subcommand list is complete: an unknown word in subcommand position is an error.
.TP
.BR options-complete " (boolean, default false)"
The option list is complete: an unknown option is an error.
.TP
.BR options-first " (boolean, default false)"
Options are recognised only before the first plain argument, and later words are plain, as in
.BI "docker run " "image command " \-x\fR.
.TP
.BR abbreviations " (boolean, default false)"
A unique prefix of a subcommand name or alias selects that subcommand, as
.B inst
does in
.BR "npm inst" .
.PP
A level inherits nothing from its parent except
.BR common-options .
Options declared in a level's
.B options
apply only at that level, so the built-in
.B git
spec recognises
.B \-\-git\-dir=
before a subcommand and treats it as unknown after
.BR commit ,
whose level does not declare it.
.SS Option syntax
Each entry in
.B options
and
.B common-options
is one spelling of one option.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose ", " \-name
A flag that takes no value.
.TP
.BR \-m= ", " \-\-message=
An option with a required value.
The value is accepted attached
.RB ( \-\-message=text ,
and for single-letter options
.BR \-mtext )
or as the next word
.RB ( "\-\-message text" ,
.BR "\-m text" ).
The value word is a plain argument.
.TP
.BR \-S=? ", " \-\-color=?
An option with an optional value, accepted only attached
.RB ( \-\-color=always ,
.BR \-Skey ).
The next word is never consumed.
.TP
.BR +* ", " \-\-no\-*
A pattern.
A trailing
.B *
matches every word that starts with the text before it.
A pattern takes no separate value.
A pattern is the only way a word that does not start with
.B \-
is an option, as
.B +nightly
is in
.BR "cargo +nightly build" .
.PP
Every other entry starts with
.BR \- ;
an entry that does not is an invalid option.
Single-letter options bundle:
.B \-av
is
.B \-a \-v
when every letter is a declared single-letter option.
A letter that takes a value ends the bundle, and the rest of the word, or else the next word, is
its value, as in
.BR "\-xzf archive.tar" .
.SS Classification
The daemon walks a command's arguments in order, starting at the top level of its spec.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-
is an option and ends option parsing; every later word is plain.
At an
.B options-first
level, every word after the first plain argument is plain,
.B \-\-
included.
.IP \(bu 2
A word that is just
.B \-
is plain, unless
.B \-
is declared as an option.
.IP \(bu 2
A word that starts with
.BR \- ,
or matches a pattern, is a known option or an unknown option.
An unknown option is an error only at a level with
.BR "options-complete = true" .
A word of the form
.BI \- digits
is never an error.
.IP \(bu 2
The first plain word at a level is in subcommand position.
A known subcommand name or alias there is reported as
.BR subcommand ,
and classification continues at that subcommand's level.
An unknown word there is an error only at a level that has subcommands and
.BR "complete = true" .
A word that contains an expansion is never an error.
.IP \(bu 2
After the first plain word at a level, no further subcommand matching happens at that level.
.SS Precommands
For a spec with
.BR "precommand = true" ,
the daemon finds the wrapped command by skipping the precommand's options and their values, with
unknown options assumed to be flags; then
.IB NAME = value
words when
.BR "skip-assignments = true" ;
then
.B positional
plain words.
The next word is the wrapped command, which is classified as a command in its own right.
Option parsing stops at
.B \-\-
or at the first word that is not an option, as POSIX
.BR getopt (3)
does.
.SS Loading and overriding
A user file whose
.B name
matches an existing spec replaces that spec entirely.
With
.B merge = true
it is merged instead: option lists and aliases are appended, with a later spelling of the same
option taking precedence; keys present in the user file override; and subcommand tables are
merged recursively by name.
Merging cannot remove anything; replacing the spec can.
A file with
.B merge = true
and no existing spec of that name is loaded as a new spec.
.PP
A file that fails to parse, has an unknown key, or declares an invalid option is skipped with a
warning, and the other files still load.
The daemon writes the warning to its log;
.B fast-highlight check-config
and
.B fast-highlight highlight
print it on standard error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IB config /config.toml
The configuration file.
.TP
.IB config /theme.toml
The theme when
.B config.toml
has no
.B theme
key.
.TP
.IB config /themes/ name .toml
The theme selected by
.B theme = \(dq\fIname\fP\(dq
in
.BR config.toml .
.TP
.IB config /specs/*.toml
User command specs.
.SH EXAMPLES
A
.B config.toml
that selects a user theme, raises the lex-only threshold, and logs request timing:
.PP
.RS
.EX
theme = "mine"
\&
[limits]
lex\-only\-bytes = 20480
hard\-cap\-bytes = 65536
\&
[log]
timing = true
file = "\(ti/.cache/fast\-highlight.log"
.EE
.RE
.PP
The theme
.BR themes/mine.toml ,
which keeps the basic-colour
.B default
theme but greys comments, restyles errors, and leaves path prefixes
unstyled:
.PP
.RS
.EX
inherits = "default"
\&
[meta]
name = "mine"
description = "Basic colours with grey comments"
\&
[styles]
comment = { fg = 244 }
error = "fg=white,bg=red,bold"
path\-prefix = ""
.EE
.RE
.PP
A spec for a command with no built-in spec, in
.BR specs/just.toml :
.PP
.RS
.EX
name = "just"
options = ["\-f=", "\-\-justfile=", "\-l", "\-\-list", "\-n", "\-\-dry\-run", "\-\-set="]
.EE
.RE
.PP
A spec that adds a subcommand to the built-in
.B git
spec without replacing it, in
.BR specs/git\-local.toml :
.PP
.RS
.EX
name = "git"
merge = true
\&
[subcommands.sync]
options = ["\-\-dry\-run"]
.EE
.RE
.PP
A spec with nested subcommands and value-taking options.
Without
.BR "merge = true" ,
a file in this form replaces the built-in
.B git
spec:
.PP
.RS
.EX
name = "git"
common\-options = ["\-h", "\-\-help"]
options = ["\-C=", "\-c=", "\-p", "\-\-paginate", "\-\-git\-dir=", "\-\-version"]
\&
[subcommands.commit]
options = ["\-m=", "\-\-message=", "\-a", "\-\-all", "\-\-amend", "\-S=?", "\-\-gpg\-sign=?"]
\&
[subcommands.remote]
complete = true
\&
[subcommands.remote.subcommands.remove]
aliases = ["rm"]
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fast-highlight (1),
.BR zshzle (1)
.PP
The source repository is
.UR https://github.com/wolpert/fast-highlight
.UE .
